¶ 60    In addition to being a claim against the State, plaintiffs’ request for attorney  fees and costs is also not a forward-looking claim for relief but is a request for a  monetary remedy to compensate plaintiffs for something that had already occurred  at the time the request was made, i.e., attorney fees and expenses incurred prior to  the circuit court’s judgment. Therefore, an award of attorney fees and costs does  not fit within the definition of prospective injunctive relief, which the appellate  court in Shempf v. Chaviano, 2019 IL App (1st) 173146, ¶ 54, correctly noted is “a  different matter altogether.” Once a plaintiff seeks to tap State funds by seeking  attorney fees and costs, that portion of the plaintiff’s request for relief is no longer  purely against the rogue officer’s conduct but seeks a monetary judgment payable  by the State itself.   
¶ 61    In Shempf, the appellate court correctly held that, despite the presence of a  general cost-shifting provision in section 10-55(c) of the Administrative Procedure  Act (5 ILCS 100/10-55(c) (West 2016)), the circuit court lacked subject-matter  jurisdiction to award costs against the State as part of the injunctive relief awarded  in that case. Schempf, 2019 IL App (1st) 173146, ¶¶ 62-63. The Shempf court, citing  this court’s precedent, correctly noted that “[s]tatutes that generally allow for fees  or costs to prevailing parties, but do not expressly refer to the State, do not waive  sovereign immunity.” Id. ¶ 65 (citing Walker, 131 Ill. 2d at 304).  
